camchrome: an Android persona's content area is the phone's width; viewport tags follow desktop-site mode.

* Chrome's desktop window has a hard 500 px minimum content width
  (BrowserViewLayout::kMainBrowserContentsMinimumWidth). 975 sizes the window to the persona's work
  area, but for a phone (360-430 CSS px) the minimum pushed it back to 500, so a
  width=device-width page laid out at innerWidth 500 next to screen.width 360 -- the viewport
  tag most mobile pages carry made the mismatch visible. The floor is dropped for a phone
  persona; the toolbar's own minimum still applies. Measured: innerWidth == clientWidth ==
  screen.width (360, 412) on width=device-width pages; 980 px layout without a tag, as on Android.

* Chrome for Android stops honouring viewport meta tags while a desktop-site override is active
  (a UA override whose metadata says not-mobile; web_contents_impl.cc). 002's mobile web-prefs
  block forced them on after that decision. It now leaves them off in that state. (No desktop
  code path creates such an override today -- CDP overrides do not go through it -- so this keeps
  the block correct rather than changing current behaviour.)

Tests: tools/fingerprint_selftest.py --platform android (android-device-width-equals-screen,
android-no-meta-layout-980).
